James wrote:
> [line numbers in error messages]
> Yes, but the editor uses that, not the programmer.  In the worst
> case, you probably do want an editor which allows you to go to a
> specific line, but even that's limited.  Once you've fixed the
> first error, the displayed line numbers are wrong. 

If you fix the errors backwards, starting from the highest number,
you'll avoid that problem. ;-)

> (But the
> editor keeps track of where they used to be, so when you go to
> the next error, you end up on the right line, even if the line
> number no longer corresponds.)
> 
> You can configure vim to display linenumbers, but I've never
> seen anyone do so on a regular basis.

I use them at least occasionally. After all, an editor is usable
for many different types of sources, not just C. For example as I
started to write some javascript I got the error messages and the
line numbers from the browser's Error Console window, I had no
commandline output available. Though in this case I also don't
need to display the numbers, just jump to the number using vi's
'G' command. Where I find line numbers helpful is when you search-
modify through large files; if you jump from match to match (using
'n' or '*') it's helpful (to me at least) to know how many lines
you skipped and where in the code you approximately are.

